  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The system relies on LLM-derived inference which, despite expert validation surveys and cross-layer consistency checks, introduces potential uncertainties in viewpoint classification.",
      "what": "The study introduces a multi-layer computational framework that uses large language models, graph analytics, and expert validation to track how interdisciplinary team members align their ideas over time. Using text extracted into the Needs-Approach-Benefits-Competition framework, the system models semantic similarities, calculates cross-domain network centrality metrics, and tracks temporal flow patterns across research presenters. Application to a case study on water insecurity in underserved Arizona communities revealed clear trajectories of increasing conceptual alignment and domain-specific influence.",
      "why": "It replaces manual or speculative assessments of team collaboration with a quantitative method for tracing how distinct scientific disciplines influence each other and reach consensus during multi-year research initiatives."
    }
